The U.S. Commerce Department is launching a new review of whether China should be treated as a market economy country, a designation that would limit how anti-dumping duties on Chinese goods are calculated. Announced in a Wednesday notice regarding an investigation into alleged dumping of Chinese aluminum foil, the department is seeking public comments on China's non-market economy status.

This review coincides with preparations for the first face-to-face meeting between President Donald Trump and China's President Xi Jinping on April 6-7 at Trump's Mar-a-Lago retreat in Florida. China's non-market economy status is a point of contention for Beijing, especially as the Trump administration develops trade policies aimed at reducing Chinese imports.

When China joined the World Trade Organization in 2001, its terms allowed other members to treat it as a non-market economy. This permitted the use of a third country's prices to assess if Chinese goods were being sold below cost. A clause allowing this practice expired on Dec. 11, 2016. China argues this means WTO partners must cease using such surrogate pricing, which has resulted in higher U.S. anti-dumping duties on its goods.

The United States and European Union did not alter their stance on China's status following the expiration. In response, Beijing launched a formal WTO complaint against them the next day.

The Commerce Department last reviewed China's non-market economy status in 2006, finding China failed all six determining criteria:
* the extent of currency convertibility;
* the extent to which wage rates are set by free bargaining;
* the extent to which foreign firms are permitted joint ventures or investments;
* the extent of government ownership or control of production means;
* the extent of government control over resource allocation and enterprise price/output decisions;
* and other factors deemed appropriate.

